WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:04.690 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  the Xbox one enjoyed a strong third year  on the market with an expanding  backwards-compatibility catalog Xbox  play anywhere support and a nice system  revision its software lineup was no  slouch either with some strong  exclusives and an even stronger array of  multi-platform games Ward's of horizon 3  is one such game building on the  foundations of the previous two horizon  titles it offers a tremendous amount of  variety for a racer it's Australian  setting provides a wide array of diverse  locations to explore letting you  seamlessly drive between the outback  jungles beaches and the countryside  there are a ton of different things to  do throughout horizon threes open-world  you might wander off the beaten path to  search for a hidden car take part in an  impromptu race smash a hard-to-reach  billboard set a new high speed or drift  record or simply stop to take in the  sights horizon 3 is one of the best  looking games of the year on any  platform  [Music]  Dishonored 2 builds on the foundation of  its predecessor with a more believable  world intelligent AI and a choice of two  playable characters each with their own  distinct skills what it lacks in terms  of difficulty and interesting  storytelling it more than makes up for  with some brilliantly conceived levels  and opportunities for experimentation  prior to release director Harvey Smith  described how play testers had combined  powers in ways that developers hadn't  considered it's easy to spend hours  playing around with the myriad  possibilities that exist in his review  GameSpot Scott Butterworth even  describes Dishonored 2 as being as much  a toy box as it is a game it's also one  of those games that sticks with you  after you're done playing for the day  it'll keep you up at night as you relive  your conquests and contemplate the  alternative approaches you could have  taken  a frequent criticism of the first  titanfall was the lack of a real  single-player mode titanfall 2 more than  answers the call providing us with a  well-paced story in some of the most  memorable moments and levels of the year  it also manages to make you genuinely  care about your Titan counterpart even  if the writing isn't as strong as it  could be this emphasis on single-player  didn't come at the expense of the strong  multiplayer that titanfall is known for  titanfall 2 introduces new progression  systems that give you ample reasons to  keep you coming back there are also  distinct new Titans including one that  can wield a sword and another that  reigns explosive fighter down on your  enemies throughout all of this titanfall  2 can also boast about having arguably  the smoothest most natural movement and  controls in any shooter on the market in  a game where you can walk around in a  huge mecha nile aiding your foes the  fact that running or wall running is  still so much fun is a testament to  respawns design work  battlefield one is another Xbox one  shooter that surprised everyone by  including a strong single-player  component alongside its quality  multiplayer mode single-player content  has never been developer dices strong  suit but by dividing battlefield ones  campaign into five disparate stories it  was able to make each storyline only as  long as it needed to be  it tells real human stories that stay  grounded by not turning characters into  superheroes and they didn't all have  happy endings  battlefield ones campaign does a far  better job of conveying the horrors of  war than any previous game in this  series its multiplayer doesn't aspire to  do the same thing instead taking the  established battlefield formula in  evolving it and that's not a bad thing  battlefield 1 offers the most dynamic  matches the series has ever seen thanks  to map dominating behemoth vehicles and  weather effects that force you to alter  your tactics  overwatch represents blizzards first  foray into shooters as it did with mmo's  collectible card games and everything  else  Blizzard offers a unique take on the  genre that streamlines a concept but not  at the expense of depth the interplay  between its 23 characters and counting  remains as interesting as it did on  launch day matches can change on the fly  not just because of players using their  Ultimates but because of the ability to  switch between characters after a death  this adds an additional layer of  strategy to what would still be an  enjoyable shooter without it one thing  that never gets old is identifying a  weakness of your team or the enemy's  team and then moving into a role that  swings the match in your favor  additional characters and maps seasonal  events and wise design changes have also  ensured the game doesn't get stale and  blizzards track records suggest that the  level of support won't fall off anytime  soon

